{"question": "A 2-year-old girl with recurrent urinary tract infections is brought to the physician for a follow-up examination. Renal ultrasound shows bilateral dilation of the renal pelvis. A voiding cystourethrography shows retrograde flow of contrast into the ureters during micturition. Histologic examination of a kidney biopsy specimen is most likely to show which of the following findings?", "options": {"A": "Diffuse interstitial infiltration with T lymphocytes", "B": "Glomerular crescents with macrophages", "C": "Matrix expansion with mesangial proliferation", "D": "Cortical thinning with tubular atrophy", "E": "Thickened glomerular capillary loops"}, "pred_answer": "A", "gold_answer": "D", "question_domains": ["Pediatrics", "Nephrology", "Urology", "Radiology", "Pathology"], "option_domains": ["Nephrology", "Urology"], "question_analyses": {"Pediatrics": "The medical scenario described involves a 2-year-old girl who has a history of recurrent urinary tract infections (UTIs) and presents with findings from imaging studies that suggest a condition known as vesicoureteral reflux (VUR). The renal ultrasound showing bilateral dilation of the renal pelvis (hydronephrosis) and the voiding cystourethrography (VCUG) demonstrating retrograde flow of contrast into the ureters during micturition are indicative of VUR, which is the abnormal backflow of urine from the bladder up into the ureters and kidneys.\n\n### Interpretation of the Condition:\n1. **Recurrent UTIs**: This is a common symptom in children with VUR, as the reflux of urine can lead to bacterial colonization of the urinary tract, increasing the risk of infections.\n2. **Bilateral Hydronephrosis**: The dilation of the renal pelvis on ultrasound suggests that both kidneys are affected, which is consistent with bilateral VUR.\n3. **Retrograde Flow on VCUG**: This confirms the diagnosis of VUR, as the imaging shows urine flowing back into the ureters during the act of urination.\n\n### Noteworthy Aspects:\n- *